Subject: [PATCH 06948/11362] [outer-loop] Task 13: generator-size A/B,
 Qwen3.6-27B-MTP vs frozen Qwen3.5-9B-MTP (REQ-ARC-WMTE-5596)

(a) Re-verified the Kaggle scored-backend hardware with more rigorous, first-party evidence:
docs.arcprize.org's starter kit names an rtx6000 accelerator (g4-standard-48, RTX Pro 6000,
96GB) as "ARC-AGI-3 exclusive," and this session's clone of the Milestone-1 3rd-place team's
real, scored kernel-metadata.json confirms it in practice (machine_shape: NvidiaRtxPro6000).
Updated docs/research-notes/arc-kaggle-accelerator-upgrade-2026-06-21.md; clarified our own
P100 probe finding is from an unrelated dev kernel, not evidence the submission kernel's
deliberate NvidiaL4 quota-cost tradeoff is broken.

(c) Direct GGUF metadata inspection found the first candidate considered (gemma-4-31B-it) has
no MTP support at all. Downloaded the better-fitting official unsloth/Qwen3.6-27B-MTP-GGUF
(genuinely MTP-capable, matching the operator's exact "qwen-3.6-27B" framing) instead. Found a
second, deeper compatibility gap via a real manual launch: llama.cpp self-draft MTP loads the
GGUF file twice (main + draft), needing ~32.6GB for this 16.3GB model -- a real CUDA OOM on a
single 24GB RTX 3090. Built a VRAM-feasibility check so the candidate correctly runs without
MTP on this hardware instead of crash-looping.

(b) Also found and fixed a structural GPU-pinning bug: the first working draft never stopped
the current arm's server before starting the candidate arm, so both models contended for the
same GPU and the candidate silently fell back to the slow iGPU with no error. Fixed via
proposer.stop() + a port-health poll in a finally block. With both bugs fixed, the real
4-attempt measurement (m0r0 + sk48, both arms, GPU 1) shows the candidate with materially
higher heldout_accuracy on both games (m0r0 0.0->0.5, sk48 0.2->1.0) -- a narrow but real
positive signal.

(d) The frozen live-submission generator is unchanged; no Kaggle config touched or pushed.

